{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Behold, your days draw near to die.</b> In Ruth Rabbah (15) it concludes: Moses said to the Holy One, blessed be He, 'With הֵן I praised You, as it is written (Deuteronomy 10:14): ״הֵן לַה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ הַשָּׁמַיִם״ — and with הֵן You decree death upon me?' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, 'Remember what you said: ״וְהֵן לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ לִי״ (Exodus 4:1).' And this midrash requires explanation — was death really decreed upon him because he said ״וְהֵן לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ לִי״?'" "And it seems to me that the matter is indeed true: that which he said, ״וְהֵן לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ לִי,״ is what caused death to be decreed upon him. For since Moses testified concerning Israel that they are not steadfast in faith, if so he should have spoken to the rock in order to strengthen hands slack in faith. For had Moses not said ״וְהֵן לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ לִי,״ I would have said that they were established in Moses' eyes as believers, and since they are strong in faith there is no difference whether he speaks to the rock or strikes it — for the essential reason for ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם אֶל הַסֶּלַע״ is so that they would learn an a fortiori argument from the rock, etc., as it is written (Numbers 20:12): ״יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי״ — it does not say אֲמַנְתֶּם but rather ״הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם,״ in the causative conjugation, meaning: you did not cause Israel to believe in Me through the rock, that they would learn from it an a fortiori argument. And if Moses had held them in the presumption of being believers, his guilt would not have been so great as to deserve a death-decree; but since he said ״וְהֵן לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ לִי,״ behold, he established them in the presumption of being non-believers, and they needed strengthening in faith — through this his guilt grew very great, that he did not speak to the rock to strengthen knees buckling in faith. Therefore it says: ״הֵן קָרְבוּ יָמֶיךָ לָמוּת״ — that same הֵן which you said, ״וְהֵן לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ לִי,״ is what caused ״קָרְבוּ יָמֶיךָ לָמוּת״.'" "Another matter: since our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 97a) that one who suspects the worthy suffers in his body — whence do we know this? From Moses, who said ״וְהֵן לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ לִי״ etc. — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: you too will end up not believing, as it says ״יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי.״ And although according to its plain sense he was afflicted with leprosy, nevertheless it seems to my eyes more correct to explain that everyone who suspects the worthy suffers in his body — meaning that he suffers in his body in the very same matter of which he suspected his fellow: he himself will be suspected of it, for whoever disqualifies is disqualified by his own blemish (Kiddushin 70a). And this is precisely the meaning of 'suffers in his body.' And a proof from Moses, who said ״וְהֵן לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ,״ and in the end suffered in his body in that very same suspicion, as it says ״יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי״ — for this midrash applies ״לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם״ to Moses himself. If so, that same הֵן brought near his end: for since he said ״וְהֵן לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ לִי,״ he brought harm upon himself, that he stumbled in what is said, ״יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי,״ and through this death was decreed upon him. And this is what is said: ״הֵן קָרְבוּ יָמֶיךָ לָמוּת.״"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The citation ברבתי (טו) refers to Ruth Rabbah 15."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:33.617512431Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}